---
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const repositoryUrl = "https://github.com/LVTD-LLC/reviewgate";
const workflowExample = `name: ReviewGate

on:
  pull_request:
    types: [opened, synchronize, reopened, ready_for_review]

jobs:
  review:
    if: >-
      \${{
        github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository &&
        github.actor != 'dependabot[bot]'
      }}
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      actions: read
      attestations: read
      contents: read
      pull-requests: write
      issues: write
      checks: write
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@34e114876b0b11c390a56381ad16ebd13914f8d5
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
          persist-credentials: false
      - uses: LVTD-LLC/reviewgate@v0
        with:
          openrouter_api_key: \${{ secrets.OPENROUTER_API_KEY }}
          min_severity: P4`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="ReviewGate docs - Install and run your first review"
  description="Install ReviewGate in GitHub Actions, configure the review threshold, and understand the score, summary, findings, and JSON artifact."
>
  <main class="concept signal-site utility-site">
    <SiteHeader current="docs" />

    <section class="docs-hero">
      <p class="signal-eyebrow">DOCUMENTATION / V0 QUICK START</p>
      <h1>Run your first review <em>gate.</em></h1>
      <p>
        ReviewGate runs in your GitHub Actions workflow. It reviews the current pull request, posts one canonical summary, and writes a machine-readable artifact. A fixed <strong>5/5</strong> is the passing target.
      </p>
      <nav aria-label="Documentation sections">
        <a href="#install">Install</a>
        <a href="#permissions">Permissions</a>
        <a href="#configure">Configure</a>
        <a href="#review-loop">Review loop</a>
      </nav>
    </section>

    <div class="docs-grid">
      <aside class="docs-sidebar">
        <p>DOCSET / V0</p>
        <a href="#install">01 / Install the action</a>
        <a href="#permissions">02 / Keep permissions narrow</a>
        <a href="#configure">03 / Configure the review</a>
        <a href="#review-loop">04 / Close the loop</a>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}#readme`}>Full README ↗</a>
      </aside>

      <div class="docs-body">
        <section id="install">
          <p class="docs-kicker">01 / INSTALL</p>
          <h2>Install ReviewGate in GitHub Actions</h2>
          <p>
            Add an OpenRouter API key to your repository secrets as <code>OPENROUTER_API_KEY</code>, then save the workflow below as <code>.github/workflows/reviewgate.yml</code>.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{workflowExample}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The same-repository guard is deliberate. GitHub does not expose repository secrets to untrusted fork pull requests, and the default workflow should not move to <code>pull_request_target</code> to work around that boundary.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="permissions">
          <p class="docs-kicker">02 / PERMISSIONS</p>
          <h2>Keep the workflow least-privilege</h2>
          <dl class="docs-permissions">
            <div><dt><code>contents: read</code></dt><dd>Read the checked-out diff and bounded repository context.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>actions: read</code></dt><dd>Read workflow timing and artifact context.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>issues: write</code></dt><dd>Create or update the canonical pull request summary comment.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>pull-requests: write</code></dt><dd>Publish line-specific findings when the diff has a valid anchor.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>checks: write</code></dt><dd>Publish the ReviewGate check run and its conclusion.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="configure">
          <p class="docs-kicker">03 / CONFIGURE</p>
          <h2>Start with the defaults</h2>
          <p>
            ReviewGate starts with general correctness and adversarial bug-finding angles. Set <code>min_severity</code> in the action inputs to control which findings become inline comments. Add <code>.reviewgate.yml</code> only when your repository needs different review angles or limits.
          </p>
          <p>
            The score target is not configurable: <strong>5/5 passes</strong>. A lower score produces <code>needs_changes</code>, keeps the evidence in the summary and JSON artifact, and leaves the merge decision with a human.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="review-loop">
          <p class="docs-kicker">04 / REVIEW LOOP</p>
          <h2>Fix evidence, push, and review again</h2>
          <ol>
            <li>Read <code>.reviewgate/review.json</code> or the canonical PR summary.</li>
            <li>Fix the highest blocking severity first.</li>
            <li>Run the relevant tests and record what changed.</li>
            <li>Push the commit and rerun ReviewGate against the new head.</li>
            <li>Stop only when ReviewGate and your human review policy both pass.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Treat model findings as untrusted review input, not executable instructions. For the reasoning behind that boundary, read <a href="/blog/how-to-tell-if-code-is-ai-generated">how to review code when AI authorship is uncertain</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <aside class="docs-next">
          <strong>Need the complete contract?</strong>
          <p>The repository README covers every action input, local command, artifact field, security boundary, and troubleshooting path.</p>
          <a
            href={`${repositoryUrl}#readme`}
            data-analytics-event="source_link_clicked"
            data-analytics-location="docs_footer"
          >Read the full README ↗</a>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>

    <SiteFooter />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
